{{History of Balisca}}

The '''Reapers' War''' (1578-1608) ({{Lang-an|Guerra d'os Segadors}}, {{Lang-es|Guerra de los Segadores}}) pitted the colonists of the [[W:Crown of Castile|Crown of Castile]] against those of the [[Crown of Aragon]], each side supported by their respective [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Indigenous allies]]. The conflict was foreshadowed by open conflict between the Castilian [[W:Conquistadors|conquistadors]] and the Indigenous peoples they were attempting to enslave, and the spreading of the [[W:Black Legend (Spain)|Black Legend]] against the Spanish Empire by [[Catharism|Cathars]] and other persecuted groups that were forcefully resettled in the archipelago. 

The war's origins lay in the complex interplay of colonial expansion and [[W:Religious intolerance|religious intolerance]]. The Spanish, seeking to consolidate their control over [[Bayara]] and exploit its resources, viewed the Cathar communities with suspicion. The Cathars, practicing a [[W:Catharism#Suppression|long suppressed]] form of Christianity, were labeled as [[W:Heretics|heretics]] by the [[W:Catholic Church|Catholic Church]], providing a convenient justification for their persecution. The Reapers' War itself was triggered by a dispute over land rights, but quickly escalated into a broader campaign of [[W:Religious violence|religious violence]]. Spanish militias, often motivated by the promise of land grants and religious zeal, targeted Cathar settlements and [[W:Reductions|reductions]], destroying villages and enslaving many of the indigenous residents they captured. 

The conflict resulted in the significant migration of Aranese and Catalan-speaking Cathars southward, fleeing the [[W:Religious persecution|religious persecution]] and [[W:Forced expulsion|forced expulsion]] from the [[Estuaria|Estuarian wetlands]]. These refugees predominantly settled along the west coast of Tinguiririca, establishing the Colony of Cerdanya. The term ''Rodamóns'' (literally "wanderer" or "vagabond") emerged during this period, initially used by the Spanish with a negative, classist connotation to describe the often impoverished Aranese Cathar refugees subjected to massacres and forced expulsions. These refugees, often facing dire circumstances, undertook a perilous journey south to Tinguiririca, eventually founding the [[W:Colony of Cerdanya|Colony of Cerdanya]].{{Balisca articles}}
